# Controllers, requests and responses

Howdy {{.Release}}. Read an existing controller in `src/{{.Name}}/features/`
first and follow it.

## A controller

```gleam
import howdy/controller
import howdy/param
import howdy/service

pub fn controller(db: Repo) {
  controller.new("api/notes")            // prefix; "" for the site root
  |> controller.get("/", fn(ctx) { ... }) // GET /api/notes
  |> controller.get("/search", search)   // fixed paths before /:id
  |> controller.get("/:id", fn(ctx) {
    use id <- param.int(ctx, "id")       // 400 if missing or not an int
    notes.find(db, id) |> service.respond(ctx, note.to_json)
  })
  |> controller.post("/", create(db, _))
}
```

- Methods: `controller.get`, `post`, `put`, `patch`, `delete`, or
  `controller.route(c, http.Head, path, handler)` for any other. GET also
  answers HEAD, and OPTIONS is answered for you.
- Paths: `:name` captures a segment; a final `*rest` captures the rest.
  Among routes without `*`, the first declared wins, so `/new` must come
  before `/:id`.
- Register it in `app` in `src/{{.Name}}.gleam`:
  `|> howdy.controller(notes_controller.controller(db))`. Pass it what it
  needs from the `Services` record{{if .Database}} (`db`, and whatever `howdy add` put there){{end}} as arguments.
- A handler is `fn(ctx) -> Response(Content)`. `ctx.request` is the
  `gleam/http` request, `ctx.params` the path parameters.
- Helpers that take a context should accept any guard:
  `fn show(ctx: controller.GuardedContext(guarded), ...)`.

## Reading the request

All of these are written for `use`, and answer the request themselves
(400, 415 or 422 JSON) when the input is bad:

```gleam
use id <- param.int(ctx, "id")                    // howdy/param: int, string
use page <- query.int_or(ctx, "page", default: 1) // howdy/query
use term <- query.optional_string(ctx, "q")       // Option(String)
use input <- body.validated(ctx, note.input_decoder(), note.validate) // howdy/body: 400 bad JSON, 422 invalid
use data <- body.json(ctx, decoder)               // decode only
use theme <- cookie.string_or(ctx, "theme", default: "system") // howdy/cookie
```

- `howdy/query` also has `string`, `int`, `bool` (only `true`/`false`),
  their `optional_` and `_or` forms, and `strings` for repeated keys.
- Headers: `request.get_header(ctx.request, "x-api-key")` from
  `gleam/http/request`.
- Form posts: `howdy/form` (`use fields <- form.read(ctx)`, then
  `form.string(fields, "name", rules, next)`){{if not .API}}, or the project's own
  `pages.read_form` if `platform/pages.gleam` exists{{end}}. Do not name a variable
  `form`: it hides the module.

## Validation (`howdy/validate`)

```gleam
pub fn validate(input: NoteInput) -> validate.Result(NewNote) {
  use body <- validate.field("body", input.body, [validate.trim(), validate.not_empty(), validate.max_length(500)])
  use rating <- validate.field("rating", input.rating, [validate.min(1), validate.max(5)])
  validate.ok(NewNote(body:, rating:))
}
```

Rules: `trim` (first, it normalises), `not_empty`, `min_length`,
`max_length`, `email`, `min`, `max`, `one_of(allowed, show)`,
`custom(check, problem)`. A rule fails with a `howdy/problem` `Problem`, not
a sentence; a rule of your own gives a `Custom` one, with a code for clients
and the English message:
`validate.custom(fn(name) { !string.contains(name, " ") }, problem.Custom("spaces", "must not contain spaces"))`.
Every field is checked and all errors are returned together, as
`service.FieldError(field, problem)`; a JSON client gets 422
`{"error":"validation failed","code":...,"fields":[{"field":...,"code":...,"message":...}]}`.
To show a problem on a page, put it into words:
`problem.message(found, problem.english())`. In tests, compare problems:
`FieldError("age", problem.TooSmall(13))`.

## Responding

Operations return `service.Result(a)`; pipe the result into a responder:

```gleam
notes.all(db) |> service.respond(ctx, json.array(_, note.to_json)) // 200
notes.create(db, input) |> service.created(ctx, note.to_json)      // 201
notes.delete(db, id) |> service.no_content(ctx)                    // 204
service.error_response(ctx, service.NotFound("no such note"))
```

| `service.Error` | Status |
| --- | --- |
| `Invalid(msg)` | 400 |
| `Unauthorized` | 401 |
| `Forbidden` | 403 |
| `NotFound(msg)` | 404 |
| `Conflict(msg)` | 409 |
| `UnsupportedMediaType(msg)` | 415 |
| `Validation(List(FieldError))` | 422 |
| `TooManyRequests(retry_after_seconds:)` | 429 |
| `Internal(msg)` | 500; logged, the client sees only "internal server error" |
| `Coded(code, error)` | the inner error's; `code` is sent to the client |

Error bodies are always `{"error": "...", "code": "..."}`: the `Coded`
error's own code, or one for the kind, such as `not_found`. Howdy's own
packages (Howdy Auth) return coded errors, so match on the kind inside with
`result.map_error(service.uncoded)` before a `case`. Other responses:
`controller.json(ctx, json)`, `controller.text`, `controller.html`,
`controller.status(ctx, 204)`, and `|> controller.with_status(422)` on any
response. There is no redirect helper in core:
`controller.status(ctx, 303) |> response.set_header("location", "/notes")`{{if not .API}},
or `pages.redirect(ctx, to: path)` where `platform/pages.gleam` exists{{end}}.
Cookies: `cookie.set(res, name, value, cookie.defaults())`, `cookie.delete`.

## Middleware

```gleam
pub fn require_api_key(ctx: controller.Context, next: controller.Next) {
  case request.get_header(ctx.request, "x-api-key") {
    Ok("secret") -> next(ctx)
    _ -> service.error_response(ctx, service.Unauthorized)
  }
}
```

Apply it to the app (`|> howdy.middleware(m)` in `src/{{.Name}}.gleam`), to a
controller (`|> controller.middleware(m)`, which covers every route), or to
one route (`controller.post("/", create |> middleware.wrap(m))` with
`howdy/middleware`).
{{- if not .API}} `src/{{.Name}}/platform/not_found.gleam` is a worked example.{{end}}

## Mistakes to avoid

- A new controller that is not registered in `app` serves nothing.
- `service.respond(ctx, ...)` is wrong: the result comes first, piped in.
- Never write on GET{{if not .API}}; HTML forms can only GET and POST, so updates
  and deletes from pages are POSTs (`/notes/:id`, `/notes/:id/delete`){{end}}.
- Database calls belong in the feature's operations module, not in the
  handler.
